<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">New Kingdom of Granada</h1><p class="st-meta">1538 CE – 1717 CE · capital Santa Fe de Bogotá</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Spanish colony created in Colombia's Andean highlands after the conquest of the Muisca, ruled from Santa Fe de Bogotá and the forerunner of Colombia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-jimenez-de-quesada">Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada</a> led an expedition up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdalena-river">Magdalena</a> river and conquered the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/muisca-confederation">Muisca</a> between 1537 and 1539. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Santa Fe de Bogotá</a> in 1538 and named the land the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> after his home region in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In 1549 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Royal Audiencia</a> was established at Santa Fe.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From 1564 the territory was governed by a president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>. Its economy depended on gold from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioquia-department">Antioquia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popayan">Popayán</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choco-department">Chocó</a>, mined increasingly by enslaved Africans, as well as emeralds from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzo">Muzo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartagena-colombia">Cartagena de Indias</a> was the main port and a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. The indigenous population fell sharply and was subject to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> and forced labour. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-claver">Peter Claver</a> ministered to enslaved Africans in Cartagena.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1717 the Spanish crown elevated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/viceroyalty-of-new-granada">Viceroyalty of New Granada</a>, separating it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and adding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Panama</a>. Its name survived in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-new-granada">Republic of New Granada</a> after independence, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> remains the capital of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-colombia">Colombia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/new-kingdom-of-granada.jpg" alt="New Kingdom of Granada" width="320" height="362"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colombia_(1810).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1538 CE – 1717 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santa Fe de Bogotá</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada, Andrés Díaz Venero de Leiva</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nuevo Reino de Granada, Presidency of Santa Fe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1628&amp;focus=new-kingdom-of-granada" aria-label="New Kingdom of Granada on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2625 2